# request: "Walk me through what happens when a shopper opens a product page, for
#  the onboarding doc. The browser sends GET /products/42 to CloudFront; on a cache
#  miss CloudFront forwards it to the ALB in our AWS VPC, which passes it to the Kong
#  API gateway. Kong routes /products to the Catalog API; it also fronts the Orders
#  and Cart APIs (all three are Go services). The Catalog API reads the Redis cache,
#  misses, and selects product 42 from the Postgres catalog DB. The reply goes back
#  the same way and CloudFront caches it for 60 seconds. Number the hops and
#  highlight the Catalog API."
type: walkthrough
reader: new engineers reading the onboarding doc
width: 800
direction: down
focus: aws.catalog
out: DNS and TLS handshakes, auth, the cache write-back, logging
nodes:
  browser: Browser
  reply: 7. The reply retraces the path\nand CloudFront caches it 60 s {note}
  cdn: CDN\nCloudFront
  aws: AWS VPC
  aws.alb: Load balancer\nALB
  aws.gw: API gateway\nKong
  aws.orders: Orders API\nGo
  aws.catalog: Catalog API\nGo
  aws.cart: Cart API\nGo
  aws.cache: Cache\nRedis {shape: cylinder}
  aws.db: Catalog DB\nPostgres {shape: cylinder}
edges:
  browser -> cdn: 1. GET /products/42
  cdn -> aws.alb: 2. cache miss
  aws.alb -> aws.gw: 3. forward
  aws.gw -> aws.orders
  aws.gw -> aws.catalog: 4. route /products
  aws.gw -> aws.cart
  aws.catalog -> aws.cache: 5. read cache (miss)
  aws.catalog -> aws.db: 6. SELECT product 42
